  "raw_text": "283\n\nWilbaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto rotate, to turn, to spin, to revolve, ...\n \n/ rotar, girar,\n \n...\n\nWilbi lakaia\n \n(\ndb\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto change or alter or make something on something that rotates\n \n/ cambiar o alterar o\nhacer algo en algo que gira\n\n(\nNote/Nota\n:\n \ndb\n-\nJ\nirimaya\n \n18:3\n \nuses to describe\n \nworking on a potter’s wheel;\n \ndb\n-\nWihta Nani\n \n16:21\n \nuses to describe\n \ngrinding\n\ngrain\n \n/\n \ndb\n-\nJ\nirimaya\n \n18:3\n \nse usa para describir\n \nel trabajo en un torno de alfarero\n;\n \ndb\n-\nWihta Nani\n \n16:21\n \nutiliza para describir\n\nla molienda de grano\n)\n\nWilin\n \n(adj\n,\nadv)\n \n–\n \nwilling, willingly\n \n/ dispuesto, de buena gana\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nmwb\n-\n01/2019\n):\n \nPal ba wilin\n \npali kan Jehova kupia l\nâ\nka daukaia\n \n–\n \nPaul was very willing to do Jehovah’s will\n \n/ Pablo estaba\n\nmuy dispuesto a hacer la voluntad de Jehová\n)\n\nUpla wilinkira\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nvolunteer\n \n/ voluntario\n\nWilinkira\n \n(adj,adv)\n \n–\n \nwilling, willingly\n \n/ dispuesto, de buena gana\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-01/2016\n):\n \nUpla wilinkira ailal\n \n–\n \nmany willing people\n \n/ muchos personas dispuestos)\n\nKupia wilinkira\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nwilling heart\n \n/ corazón dispuesto\n\nWilkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto tie up, to bind, to fasten, to connect, to relate to, to link\n \n/ atar, sujetar, conectar, relacionarse, vincularse\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\ndb\n-\nJer13:1\n):\n \nmaisam ra wilks\n \n–\n \ntie on your waist\n \n/ atarte la cintura\n)\n\nKiaubi wilkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto pick up and tie or bind, to collect and tie or bind\n \n/ recoger y amarrar, colectar y amarrar\n\nMaisa\n \nwilkaia\n \n(a/t:\n \nMaisha wilkaia\n,\n \nMaihsa wilkaia\n) (v)\n \n–\n \nto gird\n \n/ ceñir\n\nWilkan\n \n(adj)\n \n–\n \nconnected\n \n/ conectado\n\nWilkanka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nconnection, connector, fastener, tie, link\n \n/ conexión, conector, sujetador, corbata, enlace\n\nWilwaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto turn, to spin, to rotate, to revolve\n \n/ girar\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nlfb49\n):\n \nJoram trai kaikan wilwi tawaia ai trukka wal mahka waia dukiara, kuna Jehú trisba kum lulki bara ikan.\n \n–\n \nJehoram\ntried to turn around his chariot in order to get away, but Jehu shot an arrow and killed him.\n \n/ Jehoram intentó girar su carro para\nescapar, pero Jehú disparó una flecha y lo mató.)\n\nKli wilwaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto cycle, to turn again, to spin again, to rotate again, to revolve again\n \n/ girar, girar de nuevo\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu-05/20\n):\n \nPâsa ba nahki klin taki bara yawan ra kli wilwi tawisa ki?\n \n–\n \nHow is the breeze (air) becoming clean and\ncycling back (returning) to us?\n \n/ ¿Cómo se está limpiando la brisa (aire) y volviendo (regresando) a nosotros?))\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nwqu-05/20\n):\n \nNahki lî ba klin taki bara yawan ra kli wilwi tawisa ki?\n \n–\n \nHow is the water becoming clean and cycling\nback (returning) to us?\n \n/ ¿Cómo se está limpiando el agua y volviendo (regresando) a nosotros?)\n\nWil\n \n(a/t:\n \nWîl\n-\nlfb\n;\n \nWilwaika\n;\n \nMina wilwaika\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nwheel\n \n/ rueda\n\nWil sirka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nwheelchair\n \n/ silla de ruedas\n\nWilwaika\n \n(a/t:\n \nWîl\n-\nlfb\n;\n \nWil\n;\n \nMina wilwaika\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nwheel\n \n/ rueda\n\nWin\n \n(v-3p-past/pasado of/de:\n \nWiaia\n)\n \n–\n \nsaid, told /\n \ndijo\n\nWin takaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto win (at a game or in a battle)\n \n/ ganar (en un juego o en una batalla)\n\n(Note/Nota: This is used in\n \ndb-1 Timoti 6:19\n \nto translate of “\nto get a firm hold on\n”. However,\n \nPrak alkbia\n \n(to grap, to grip...)\n \nis\nperhaps a better translation.\n \nSee\n \nbh\n-\npg192\n \n/ Esto se usa en\n \ndb\n-\n1\n \nTim\noti\n \n6:19\n \npara traducir\n \n\"para obtener un agarre firme\"\n.\n \nSin\n\nembargo,\n \nPrak alkbia\n \n(agarrar...) es\n \ntal vez\n \nuna mejor traducción. Ver\n \nbh\n-\npg192\n)\n\n(\nEx/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n08/19\n):\n \nDîa daukaia\n \nsa ki\n \nplapanka ba win takaia dukiara?\n \n–\n \nWhat must we do in order to win the race?\n \n/ ¿Qué\n\ndebemos hacer para ganar la carrera?\n)\n\nWin takanka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nvictory, win\n \n/ victoria, triunfo\n\nWîna\n \n(Wina)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nflesh, meat, body, self\n \n/ carne, cuerpo, yo, uno mismo\n\n(Construct:\n \nWîni\n;\n \nWînam\n;\n \nAi wîna\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nhf-5\n):\n \nUpla nani ba marit taki piua ba wina “w\nâl ba ai wîna kumi\n \nbaku” takisa\n \n–\n \nFrom the time that people are getting\n\nmarried, they become “the two like just one flesh”\n \n/ Desde el momento en que las personas se casan, se convierten en \"los dos\ncomo una sola carne\")\n\nAi wîna\n \n–\n \nhimself, herself, theirself\n \n/ él mismo\n\nAi wîna alki takaskan lâka ba briaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto have self-control over their body, to have control over\n \n/\n\nthemselves\n \n/ tener autocontrol sobre su cuerpo, tener control sobre sí mismos\n\n(Ex/Ej\n(\nmwb-05/20\n):\n \nJosep ba ai muihnika nani ra kaikan taim, ai wîna alki takaskan lâka ba brin.\n \n–\n \nWhen Joseph\nsaw his brothers, he had control over himself.\n \n/ Cuando José vio a sus hermanos, tenía control sobre sí mismo.)\n\nAi wîna ra baman luki\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nonly thinking of oneself, being selfish\n \n/ solo pensando en uno mismo, siendo\negoísta\n\nBîp wîna\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nbeef meat\n \n/ carne de res\n\nBunhka\n \n(a/t:\n \nBuhnka, Wîna bunhka, Wîna buhnka\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nmuscle\n \n/ músculo\n\nLih\n \nwîn\na\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nturtle meat\n \n/ carne de tortuga",
